## How to learn from the result

- **Faster evaluator score, still 5/5:** evidence the stronger tracking reward helped this run. A single run is promising, but not enough to establish a reliable improvement.
- **Higher tracking reward in TensorBoard, same or slower evaluator score:** the policy learned to optimize that training signal, but the change did not improve sprint performance. Reward curves are not the competition score.
- **Fewer than 5 finishes or much worse score:** the extra speed pressure may have hurt stability. Note which checkpoints and command speeds failed.
- **A sharp curve drop around a curriculum change:** check whether the evaluator score also worsened there; that helps distinguish a training transition from a useful checkpoint.

Write down the hypothesis, commit hash, seed, best checkpoint, evaluator score, finishes, command speed, and what the curves showed. Then decide whether to keep the change as a useful finding or try the next single-variable hypothesis. Do not publish or enter a policy without explicit approval.